    {
      "name": "Press start",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "option",
      "description": "The title's call to action: B-coloured words that blink on a stepped loop.",
      "html": "<button class=\"ds-press\" type=\"button\">PRESS START</button>",
      "css": ".ds-press{padding:12px 16px;font:400 16px 'Press Start 2P',ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--pb,#f5a524);background:none;border:0;cursor:pointer;animation:ds-blink 1.06s steps(1) infinite;}@keyframes ds-blink{50%{opacity:0;}}@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.ds-press{animation:none;}}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Console Screen",
    "overview": "Every screen is a framed pixel screen on a near-black ground, steered by a cursor. The level's two-colour palette owns the whole screen: frames, plates, the logo band, the touch pads, the blocks and the ground tint all recolour together when the level changes. The page is a game that happens to run in a browser, not a site about one: menus are words and an arrow, and the only chrome is the frame.\n\nDensity is low and centred in menus (one column, one question per screen) and tight and instrumented in the match (two wells, stat boxes, a lead meter). One typeface, one bitmap grid, flat colour layers; nothing is blurred, rounded or glowing. Motion is split in two grammars: eased slides for layout, stepped on/off for anything made of pixels.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "The level palette (A, B, ground, frame, pad disc) is the only chroma; neutrals and win/lose carry the rest.",
      "Press Start 2P for every word, at weight 400, with no smoothing.",
      "Frames are 9x9 pixel border-images, not CSS borders or radii.",
      "Menu options are bare words with a pixel arrow cursor; hover and focus both move the cursor.",
      "Everything is sized in multiples of one pixel unit (`--px`: 3px, 2px on phones)."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Palette Owns the Screen Rule",
        "body": "Components read --pa, --pb, --frame and --btn; they never hard-code a hue. A new colour in a component is a bug unless it is a neutral or win/lose.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lead Colour Rule",
        "body": "Green and coral mean ahead and behind, and appear only where the lead is shown. They are not accents.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Block Style Rule",
        "body": "I, O, T are a white core in A; J, L are solid A; S, Z are solid B. Highlights are a 1-pixel shine at the top left of a 7x7 body on an 8x8 grid.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Face Rule",
        "body": "Any new word is Press Start 2P. No system display face, no italics, no bold.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Layer Rule",
        "body": "Separate things with another flat colour or a pixel line. If a new element needs a shadow to read, change its colour layer instead.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** colour new accents from `--pa` / `--pb` and let `applyPalette` drive them, so they follow the level.",
      "**Do** build new frames, plates and cards from `.frame` and size every dimension as a multiple of `--px`.",
      "**Do** keep menus to words plus a cursor; promote a single action to the primary plate.",
      "**Do** author new icons as bitmaps in `currentColor`, 7 px tall where possible.",
      "**Do** give any new animation a reduced-motion path, and use `steps(1)` for pixel-built motion.",
      "**Do** show state with a mark (star, pip, meter) before adding text."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use border-radius, gradients, blur, glow or an elevation shadow.",
      "**Don't** add a second typeface, a bold, or type below the 10px label size.",
      "**Don't** hard-code a hue in a component, or use win green and lose coral as decoration.",
      "**Don't** add button chrome, tabs or a card grid to menus; options stay bare words.",
      "**Don't** use the Tetris name, logo or artwork, or copy NES palettes or screens: the ten pairs are original."
    ]
  }
}